<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The App Sandbox is OFF for this distribution, and that is a decision rather
  than an oversight.

  Openote ships as a directly-downloaded .dmg, not through the App Store, so
  the sandbox is optional here. Two things the app does are incompatible with
  it as this file was configured:

    * It opens notebooks at arbitrary paths the user chose — a Dropbox folder,
      a NAS mount, a self-hosted Nextcloud directory — and REMEMBERS them
      across launches. A sandboxed app can only reach a path the user picked in
      an open panel during that session unless it holds a security-scoped
      bookmark for it. That is a feature to build, not a plist key.
    * It fetches a subscribed .ics timetable over HTTPS, which needs
      com.apple.security.network.client. That key was not here either.

  Flutter's template ships app-sandbox = true and this file inherited it. The
  released .dmg has nonetheless worked, because the release workflow's ad-hoc
  `codesign --force --deep` re-sign silently DROPPED the entitlements — so the
  shipped app has always been unsandboxed by accident, while a locally-built
  one was sandboxed and most likely could not reach the user's notebooks or
  their timetable at all. macOS has never been run by a human on this project
  (see TESTING.md), which is why nobody had hit it.

  Stating the intent here makes a local build and a CI build behave the same
  way, and stops the workflow depending on a side effect of `--deep`.

  If Openote is ever submitted to the App Store the sandbox comes back on, and
  brings security-scoped bookmarks with it. That is the migration; nothing here
  forecloses it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- The subscribed timetable is an outbound HTTPS GET (ADR-0006 §8). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
